course: politics
lesson: 17
title: UK elections and the prime minister, as reported
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: In the UK you do not vote for the prime minister directly. The country is split into about 650 areas, each area picks one MP, and the leader of the party with the most MPs usually becomes prime minister. It is like each class electing a rep, and the group with the most reps choosing the head of the school council.
explain: In each area the person with the most votes wins and second place gets nothing, a system called "first past the post". So a party can win lots of votes but few seats, or lots of seats with about a third of the votes.
explain: In recent years the UK has had a lot of news: the vote to leave the European Union, three prime ministers in a few months, a landslide election in 2024 and new parties rising in the polls. This lesson tells what was reported and what each side says.
explain: The King wears the crown and reads the King's Speech, but the elected government writes it and makes the real decisions.
explain: Britain talks about it in a hundred accents: a Geordie says "howay, man" (come on) and calls a good thing "canny", a Scot says "dreich" for grey drizzly weather and "dinnae ken" for "don't know", a Londoner says "bare" for lots, and in Bristol "gurt" means great or big.
fact: In 2016 the UK held a referendum on leaving the European Union, a group of countries sharing trade rules; just over half of those who voted chose to leave, and the UK left in January 2020.
fact: Some say Brexit let the UK make its own laws and control who comes in; others say it made trade harder and cost money; Ireland stayed in the EU, which made the Northern Ireland border a big puzzle.
fact: People vote for an MP, a Member of Parliament, for their local area; if a party picks a new leader between general elections, that person can become prime minister without a general election.
fact: It was reported that Boris Johnson stepped down in 2022 after many of his own ministers quit; Liz Truss followed, then Rishi Sunak, all from the Conservative Party, also called the Tories.
fact: News reports said that after Liz Truss's government announced big tax cuts in a "mini-budget" (a quick plan for taxes) without saying how they would be paid for, borrowing money got more expensive for the government and for families; she resigned after about 50 days, the shortest time of any UK prime minister.
fact: Prime ministers can choose when to call a general election, as long as it is within five years of the last one; it was reported that Rishi Sunak announced the July 2024 election outside 10 Downing Street in pouring rain.
fact: News reports said the Labour Party won the July 2024 election with a very large majority after 14 years of Conservative governments, and Keir Starmer became prime minister after meeting the King.
fact: It was reported that Labour won lots of seats with only about a third of the votes, while Reform UK got around one in seven votes but only five seats, because of first past the post.
fact: Proportional representation is a different system in which a party's share of seats matches its share of votes.
fact: News reports in 2025 said Reform UK, led by Nigel Farage, had been doing well in opinion polls, sometimes ahead of Labour and the Conservatives; polls are guesses, not results.
fact: At Prime Minister's Questions, held every week while Parliament sits, MPs question the prime minister, the opposition challenges the government, and the Speaker keeps order.
fact: The King is head of state in a constitutional monarchy: he does not run the country or take sides, and at the King's Speech he reads out the government's plans.
fact: News reports in 2025 said the UK government planned to lower the voting age to 16 for general elections; 16- and 17-year-olds can already vote in some elections in Scotland and Wales, and the change needs a new law.
fact: Some say 16-year-olds will live longest with today's decisions and should vote; others say 18 is the clear age of adulthood.
exercise: What was Brexit? | The UK leaving the European Union | A new British breakfast | A UK election in 2024 | The King's Speech
exercise: In which year did the UK vote in the referendum on leaving the EU? | 2016 | 2020 | 2024 | 2010
exercise: In the UK, who do people vote for at a general election? | An MP for their local area | The prime minister directly | The King, who then picks the MPs | The Speaker of the House of Commons
exercise: How can someone become prime minister without a general election? | Their party picks them as its new leader | The King picks anyone he likes | They win a TV debate | They top an opinion poll
exercise: Which three prime ministers did the UK have in 2022? | Boris Johnson, Liz Truss and Rishi Sunak | Keir Starmer, Rishi Sunak and Nigel Farage | Boris Johnson, Keir Starmer and Liz Truss | Liz Truss, Nigel Farage and Rishi Sunak
exercise: Why did Liz Truss's time as prime minister become a record? | She resigned after about 50 days, the shortest ever | She stayed for 50 years, the longest of anyone in history | She was the first woman prime minister | She was never in Parliament
exercise: What was reported to have happened after the 2022 mini-budget? | Borrowing costs went up | Prices in the shops fell to zero | An election was called the same day | The EU invited the UK back in at once
exercise: How long can a UK government go before a general election must be held? | Up to five years | Exactly one year, no more | Ten years at the very least | As long as it likes
exercise: Who became prime minister after the July 2024 election? | Keir Starmer | Rishi Sunak, again | Nigel Farage of Reform | Boris Johnson
exercise: What does a majority mean in Parliament? | Having more than half of all the MPs | Having the oldest MPs | Winning one seat | Having the most posters
exercise: What is first past the post? | In each area the candidate with the most votes wins | Seats are shared out by each party's share of all the votes | The first person to vote wins | The fastest runner becomes MP
exercise: Why did Reform UK win only five seats with about one in seven votes? | Its votes were spread thinly and second place wins nothing | Its votes were not counted, because it was a new party and had not paid the fee | It did not stand in any area | The King took its seats
exercise: What is proportional representation? | A system where a party's seats match its share of votes | A system where the biggest party in the country gets all of the seats | A system with no voting | A system for picking the King
exercise: What is Prime Minister's Questions? | A weekly session where MPs question the prime minister | A quiz show on TV | A poll of voters | A test for new MPs
exercise: Who keeps order in the House of Commons? | The Speaker | The King, from his throne | The prime minister | The loudest MP in the room
exercise: What does the King do at the King's Speech? | Reads out the plans written by the government | Writes his own laws | Picks the next prime minister | Votes on every bill
exercise: What did the government announce in 2025 about the voting age, as reported? | A plan to lower it to 16 for general elections | A plan to raise it to 21 | A plan to let 10-year-olds vote | A plan to end voting
test: Grace in Leeds: "a new PM? when did we vote for her??" Her dad says "we didn't, not exactly". What does he mean? | Her party chose her as leader, and the PM comes from the party with the most MPs | Nobody in the UK ever votes for anything, so the King picks a new leader whenever he likes | The King picked her at random | She won a TV talent show
test: Grandad in Belfast says "Britain left the club." What club? | The European Union | The United Nations in New York | A local football club | The Commonwealth Games
test: «three PMs in one year, that's bare chaos innit» (bare = lots of). What happened? | Boris Johnson stepped down, then Liz Truss, then Rishi Sunak | Three elections were held in one year, and a different party won each time | The King changed the rules | Three parties shared the job
test: "Howay man, seven weeks as PM? That cannot be right!" says a Geordie uncle (howay = come on). What does the record show about Liz Truss? | She resigned after about 50 days, the shortest of any UK prime minister | She stayed ten years | She never became prime minister, as she lost the vote of her own party's members to Sunak | She was removed by the King
test: Sunak announced the election in pouring rain, and a mate says "proper dreich, he had to do it on that day by law" (dreich = grey and wet). What is true? | Prime ministers choose the date, as long as it is within five years | The law fixes the date to the rainiest day | The King chooses the weather | Elections are always held on the first Thursday in July, by law, whatever the weather is
test: Ethan's dad in Kent: "Reform got one in seven votes but five seats, that doesn't add up!" Why? | First past the post gives nothing to second place in each area | Most of their votes were lost in the post and arrived after the count was over | They chose not to take seats | Seats are given by age
test: «Labour won a massive majority so like everyone voted for them» What is wrong? | Labour won about a third of the votes; first past the post turned that into lots of seats | Every voter chose Labour | Labour got fewer seats than votes | The Tories won more votes than Labour, but the Speaker gave Labour the extra seats instead
test: Ruby's gran says Reform will "never win"; her uncle says "watch this space". What is fair about polls? | A poll is a snapshot of today, not the final score | Polls are the final result | Polls are always wrong | Gran's guess is a poll
test: Priya sees MPs jeering at PMQs: "why can grown-ups act like that??" What do some people say in its defence? | Leaders face hard questions in public, and noise is part of a lively debate | MPs are paid extra to shout, and the loudest one gets a bonus at the end of the year | Nobody ever questions the prime minister | The Speaker leads the cheering
test: "Alright my lover, so the King's in charge then?" asks a Bristol granny (alright my lover = hello, friendly). What is the right answer? | No, the King reads the speech but the elected government decides | Yes, the King writes every law himself and the MPs just clap when he has finished | Yes, he chooses all MPs | No, the Speaker is king
test: Mia's brother, 16, says "Finally!" about the plan to let 16-year-olds vote. What would have to happen first? | Parliament would have to pass a new law | Mia's dad would have to agree | The King would have to vote | Nothing, it already applies everywhere
exam: Identify the person or persons for whom an elector in the United Kingdom casts a vote at a general election. | A Member of Parliament representing the local constituency | The Prime Minister, elected directly by the nation for a term of five years | The Sovereign | The Speaker of the House of Commons
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the system of voting known as "first past the post"? | In each constituency the candidate receiving the greatest number of votes is elected, and no seat is awarded for second place | Seats are allocated in proportion to the national share of votes, each party receiving one seat for every fraction of the vote obtained | The candidate who votes first is elected | Each party receives an equal number of seats
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the tenure of Liz Truss as Prime Minister, as reported. | She resigned after approximately fifty days, the shortest tenure of any Prime Minister of the United Kingdom | She served for a period of fifty months, the longest tenure of any Prime Minister since the Second World War | She was removed from office by the Sovereign | She was elected Prime Minister at a general election in 2022
exam: Determine the role of the Sovereign in respect of the King's Speech delivered in Parliament. | The Sovereign reads aloud the programme prepared by the elected government | The Sovereign composes the programme personally, the government reading it aloud on his behalf | The Sovereign votes upon each proposal contained therein | The Sovereign appoints the members of the House of Commons
